poet. ''thec'', instr. ''thē''; pl. nom. ''gē'' (ye), gen. ''eówer'' (your), dat. ''eów'' (you), acc. ''eów'', poet. ''eówic'' (you); dual. nom. ''git'', gen. ''incer'', dat. ''inc'', acc. ''inc'', ''incit'') = OS. ''thū'' = OFries. ''thu'' = MD. ''du'' (mod. D. uses the pl. ''gij'', = E. ''ye'', for sing.) = MLG. LG. ''du'' = OHG. MHG. ''du'', ''dū'', G. ''du'' = Icel. ''thū'' = Sw. Dan. ''du'' = Goth. ''thu'' = W. ''ti'' = Gael. Ir. ''tu'' = OBulg. ''ti'' = Russ. ''tui'', etc., = L. ''tu'' = Gr. σύ, Doric τύ = Skt. ''tvam'', thou, orig. *''tva'', one of the orig. Indo-Eur. personal pronouns (cf. ''I'', ''he'', ''the''<sup>1</sup>, ''that'', etc.). Hence ''thine'', ''thy''.] A personal pronoun of the second person, in the singular number, nominative case, the possessive case being ''thy'' or ''thine'', and the objective ''thee'': plural, ''ye'' or ''you'', ''your'', ''you''. See ''thine'' and ''you''.

{{smaller block|Wel sone, bute ''thu'' flitte,<br>With swerde ihc ''the'' anhitte.
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Thi'' soule with synne is goostly slayn,<br>And ''thou'' withoute sorewe ''thi'' synne tellis.
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 199.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Thou'' 'rt fallen again to ''thy'' dissembling trade.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, iv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|"O what dost ''thee'' want of me, wild boar," said he.
{{right|''Jovial Hunter of Bromsgrove'' (Child's Ballads, VIII. 146).}}}}

{{smaller block|I beg ''thee'' by the Filial Love<br>Due to ''thy'' Father. ''Congreve'', Hymn to Venus.}}

{{smaller block|O ''thou''! bold leader of the Trojan bands,<br>And ''you'', confed'rate chiefs from foreign lands!
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xii. 69.}}}}

{{smaller block|In ordinary English use the place of ''thou'' has been taken by ''you'', which is properly plural, and takes a plural verb. ''Thou'' is now little used except archaically, in poetry, provincially, in addressing the Deity, and by the Friends, who usually say not ''thou'' but ''thee'', putting a verb in the third person singular with it: as, ''thee'' is or is ''thee''?}}

readings.c2s4:
¶
{{smaller block|O ''thou'' that hearest prayer, unto ''thee'' shall all flesh come.
{{right|Ps. lxv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|The priest asked me, “Why we said ''Thou'' and ''Thee'' to people? for he counted us but fools and idiots for speaking so.” I asked him “Whether those that translated the scriptures, and made the grammar and accidence, were fools and idiots, seeing they translated the scriptures so, and made the grammar so, ''Thou'' to one, and ''You'' to more than one, and left it so to us?” ''George Fox'', Journal, 1665.}}

{{smaller block|And if ''thou'' marries a good un I'll leäve the land to ''thee''.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Northern Farmer, N. S.}}}}

{{smaller block|Formerly it was used in general address, and often bore special significance, according to circumstances, as noting—(''a'') equality, familiarity, or intimacy; (''b'') superiority on the part of the speaker; (''c'') contempt or scorn for the person addressed (see ''thou'', ''v.'').}}

{{smaller block|I will begin at ''thy'' heel, and tell what ''thou'' art by inches, ''thou'' thing of no bowels, ''thou!'' ''Shak.'', T. and C., ii. 1. 54.}}

